To safely migrate your SQL database during a production incident, I've broken down a step-by-step plan: First Actions:
1. Stop all database traffic immediately to avoid corrupting data in transit or causing inconsistency. Use: ```sql
SET NOEXEC = ON;
SELECT 'Stop all database connections';
GO KILL DBID; -- Replace 'DBID' with your actual database ID
``` 2. Connect to the master (primary) instance directly, not via secondary nodes. 3. Take a full backup of both databases before making changes: ```sql
BACKUP DATABASE YourDatabase TO DISK = N'C:\Program Files\Microsoft SQL Server\MSSQL12.MSSQLSERVER\MSSQL\DATA\YourDatabase.bak';
GO BACKUP LOG WITH NO_COMPRESSION TO DISK = N'C:\Program Files\Microsoft SQL Server\MSSQL12.MSSQLSERVER\MSSQL\DATA\YourDatabase.LOG'
FOR DATE @END;
``` 4. Create a script that will apply your migration changes in an orderly, reversible fashion: ```sql
SET NOEXEC = ON;
SELECT 'Begin your migration script as follows': -- Here would go the safe steps to move data and schema from the source to target tables. GO ALTER TABLE YourNewTable
ADD CONSTRAINT PrimaryKeyConstraint PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (ColumnName); -- Replace 'YourNewTable' and 'ColumnName' with actual names. GO
``` 5. Review your script in detail before running it: ```sql
SELECT 'Run your migration script against a dev or test environment first to ensure it works as expected.';
``` Second Actions:
6. Run the migration script in production carefully, validating each step, and using transaction control judiciously. 7. If you hit issues, don't be afraid to roll back changes incrementally, while still keeping your full backup intact. This minimizes data loss risk. Finally, for ongoing maintenance: 8. Always perform database backups before making any major structural changes.
9. Use transactions to isolate migrations from other work and avoid cascading effects if something goes wrong.
10. Test migrations thoroughly on a replica before applying them live to minimize risk. By following these steps you can migrate your SQL databases safely, minimizing downtime and data loss while still taking advantage of the opportunities to update your schema.
